.TH UDPCHAINS 1 "2026-08-20" "udpchains 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
udpchains \- route a program's UDP traffic through a SOCKS5 proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udpchains
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIprogram\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udpchains
runs
.I program
with an
.BR LD_PRELOAD (8)
shim that redirects its
.B UDP
traffic through a SOCKS5 proxy supporting
.BR "UDP ASSOCIATE" " (RFC 1928 \[sc]7)."
The program is not modified or recompiled.
.PP
Unlike
.BR proxychains (1),
which is TCP\-only and lets UDP go direct with no error,
.B udpchains
.B fails closed:
if a datagram cannot be proxied the call fails with an appropriate
.IR errno .
It never falls back to sending directly.
.PP
There is no built\-in default proxy. If none is configured,
.B udpchains
prints a diagnostic and refuses to run the program rather than running it
unproxied.
.PP
There is no default DNS resolver either.
Name lookups are sent through the proxy to the resolver named by
.BR UDPCHAINS_DNS ;
without one the program still runs, but any lookup that needs DNS fails.
See
.BR "NAME RESOLUTION" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-p " addr"
Proxy address, as \fIhost:port\fR or \fI[v6]:port\fR.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: errors only.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: log protocol steps. Never logs payload bytes.
.TP
.B \-V
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Show help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Configuration is resolved in precedence order: command line, environment,
configuration file.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_PROXY
Proxy as \fIhost:port\fR.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_CONFIG
Configuration file path.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_MAX_DATAGRAM
Maximum payload in bytes. Default 8192. Payloads above this fail with
.BR EMSGSIZE .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_STRICT_LOOPBACK
Promote the loopback\-destination warning to an error.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
Timeout in milliseconds for each step of the proxy handshake, not for the
whole of it.
Default 5000.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
Resolver for name lookups, as a numeric \fIip\fR, \fIip:port\fR or
\fI[v6]:port\fR (default port 53).
Queries to it are sent through the proxy.
There is no default; see
.BR "NAME RESOLUTION" .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS_TIMEOUT
Timeout for a whole name lookup, in milliseconds.
Default 5000.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS_SEARCH
Search domains, separated by spaces or commas, tried for a name with no dot.
None by default:
.I /etc/resolv.conf
is not read.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_HOSTS_FILE
Hosts file consulted before DNS.
Default
.IR /etc/hosts .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_LIB
Explicit path to \fIlibudpchains.so\fR.
.TP
.BR UDPCHAINS_USERNAME ", " UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_FILE
Credentials for RFC 1929 username/password authentication. Never accepted on
the command line, because
.I argv
is visible in
.BR ps (1).
Method
.B 0x02
is offered only when credentials are configured. GSSAPI is not implemented.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD
The password itself.
Everything the program starts inherits it; prefer
.BR UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_FILE .
.TP
.BR UDPCHAINS_QUIET ", " UDPCHAINS_DEBUG
As
.B \-q
and
.BR \-v .
Each takes a boolean:
.BR 1 / 0 ,
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false
or
.BR on / off .
.PP
.B udpchains
sets
.B UDPCHAINS_RESOLVED
and
.B UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_CONFIG
for the program it runs.
They are not meant to be set by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.udpchains.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/udpchains.conf
Configuration, as \fIkey = value\fR lines.
The first of
.BR \-f ,
.BR UDPCHAINS_CONFIG ,
.I ~/.udpchains.conf
and
.I /etc/udpchains.conf
that exists is read, and no other.
.IP
Keys:
.BR proxy ,
.BR max_datagram ,
.BR strict_loopback ,
.BR connect_timeout ,
.BR dns ,
.BR dns_timeout ,
.BR dns_search ,
.BR hosts_file ,
.BR username ,
.B password_file
(relative to the current directory),
.B password
(inline; the file must then not be readable by others),
.B quiet
and
.BR debug .
Each matches the environment variable of the same name.
An unknown key is an error.
.IP
Comments are whole lines only, beginning with
.B #
or
.BR ; .
A
.B #
after a value, following whitespace, is an error rather than part of the
value; a
.B #
inside a value is kept.
.SH NAME RESOLUTION
glibc's resolver makes its socket calls through internal functions that no
preloaded library can see, so
.B udpchains
answers
.BR getaddrinfo (3),
.BR getnameinfo (3),
.BR gethostbyname (3)
and the
.BR res_query (3)
family itself.
A numeric address answers itself;
.I localhost
and names under it are loopback; then the hosts file; then a DNS query to the
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
resolver, sent through the proxy.
An answer too large for UDP is repeated over TCP with SOCKS5 CONNECT.
.PP
The host's resolver is never used.
Without a configured resolver a lookup that needs DNS fails with
.BR EAI_FAIL ;
with an unreachable proxy it fails with
.BR EAI_AGAIN .
Other name services in
.BR nsswitch.conf (5),
such as mDNS and
.BR systemd-resolved (8),
are skipped.
Non-ASCII names and
.BR getaddrinfo_a (3)
are refused.
.PP
Programs with their own resolver, such as
.BR dig (1),
send ordinary UDP to the nameserver in
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
which is proxied like any other datagram.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran and exited successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, proxy or shim\-loading failure. The program was not run.
.TP
.B 126
The program could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
The program was not found.
.SH SECURITY
.B udpchains
keeps a program's UDP from bypassing the proxy.
It does not make the path to the proxy private.
.PP
RFC 1929 credentials cross the network in cleartext.
Datagrams to and from the proxy's relay are neither authenticated nor
encrypted: anyone on that path can read them, and a packet forged to appear
to come from the relay can claim any source address.
Reach a remote proxy over a VPN you trust, or use one on this host; an SSH
port forward carries the control connection but not the UDP relay traffic.
.PP
Report vulnerabilities privately, as described in
.IR SECURITY.md .
.SH LIMITATIONS
Cannot help statically\-linked binaries, Go binaries, or programs making
system calls without libc: there is no libc call to interpose on.
Calls made through libc's
.BR syscall (2)
are routed to the hooks. Refuses setuid, setgid and
capability\-endowed binaries, because the loader ignores
.B LD_PRELOAD
for them and running them would mean running them unproxied.
.PP
.B Raw and packet sockets are refused
with
.BR EPERM ,
as they are without
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
.PP
.B io_uring
sends go straight to the kernel and bypass the shim.
On a UDP socket,
.BR sendfile (2),
.BR splice (2)
and
.BR fdopen (3)
are refused with
.BR EINVAL ;
.BR dprintf (3)
and
.BR pwritev2 (2)
are proxied, and a socket copied with
.BR pidfd_getfd (2)
is tracked. See
.I DECISIONS.md
D\-36.
.PP
TCP is not proxied. Multicast and broadcast fail with
.BR ENETUNREACH .
A loopback destination is resolved at the proxy, so with a remote proxy it
reaches the proxy host's loopback, not this one.
.PP
Ancillary data
.RB ( msg_control )
is returned empty.
.PP
UDP servers do not work: a server's socket is pointed at the proxy's relay,
which its clients cannot reach.
.PP
.B MSG_MORE
and a
.B UDP_SEGMENT
control message are refused with
.BR EOPNOTSUPP .
.BR UDP_CORK ,
.B UDP_SEGMENT
and
.B UDP_GRO
set with
.BR setsockopt (2)
corrupt data rather than failing.
The first send or receive on a socket blocks while the proxy handshake runs,
even on a non\-blocking socket.
.B strict_loopback
is checked only when a socket's association is set up, and only for a
connected socket.
A socket shared with another process is re\-pointed for both.
.PP
After
.BR fork (2),
a child using an inherited socket is given a private one; if the parent still
holds its bound port, the child sends from another port.
A proxy given by hostname is reached at its first address only.
A non\-NULL invalid pointer inside a message faults in the shim instead of
returning
.BR EFAULT .
.PP
Sending from a signal handler is safe only on a socket already in use.
.PP
Tested against real proxies on the same host only: the reference proxy, Dante,
3proxy, gost, Xray, sing-box, hev\-socks5\-server and shadowsocks\-rust.
Some proxies silently truncate datagrams larger than their buffer, below the
default
.BR max_datagram ;
set it no higher than the proxy's limit.
See the README for the full list, and what each limitation means in practice.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Resolve a name through the proxy.
.BR dig (1)
queries the resolver it is given itself, so no
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
is needed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 dig @9.9.9.9 example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a program that looks up a name through libc, with the lookup also sent
through the proxy:
.PP
.RS
.EX
UDPCHAINS_DNS=9.9.9.9 udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 nc \-u example.com 53
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm the traffic really takes the tunnel \- this should report the proxy's
egress address, not the local one:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 dig +short myip.opendns.com @208.67.222.222
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proxychains (1),
.BR ld.so (8),
.BR socket (7),
.BR udp (7)
.PP
RFC 1928, section 7.
.SH AUTHORS
Implemented clean\-room from RFC 1928 and the relevant manual pages.
